er1)\n5. Ten-thousands:4 (carryover1)\n6. Hundred-thousands:6\n7. Millions:9\n8. Ten-millions:9\n9. Hundred-millions:0 (carryover1)\n10. Billions:1\n\nThus, the digits are: 1 (billions), 0 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 1 (ones). Wait that makes 1,099,642,931. So the correct answer is **1,099,642,931**. \n\nThe initial digit-by-digit had an error in the ten-thousands place. Let me redo it properly:\n\n**Corrected Reasoning:**\n1. Ones place: 7 + 4 = 11 → 1, carryover 1.\n2. Tens: 5 + 7 + 1 = 13 → 3, carryover 1.\n3. Hundreds: 6 + 2 + 1 = 9 → 9.\n4. Thousands: 9 + 3 = 12 → 2, carryover 1.\n5. Ten-thousands: 8 + 5 + 1 = 14 → 4, carryover 1.\n6. Hundred-thousands: 2 + 3 + 1 = 6 → 6.\n7. Millions: 7 + 2 = 9 → 9.\n8. Ten-millions: 7 + 2 = 9 → 9.\n9. Hundred-millions: 9 + 1 = 10 → 0, carryover 1.\n10. Billions: 1 (carryover).\n\nThus, the sum is **1,099,642,931**.\n\n# Answer:.\nTherefore, the final computed sum is **1,099,642,931**.", '']
