messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 341658575 and 865412687?\n# Reasoning:", '<think>Okay, so I need to add these two big numbers: 883,709,924 and 456,691,011. Let me start by writing them down one on top of the other to align the digits properly. Hmm, let\'s see. Starting from the rightmost digit, which is the ones place. \n\nFirst, the ones place: 1 + 8. That should be 86. Wait, right, so I write down 4 and carry over 4 to the tens place. \n\nNext, the tens place: 9 (from the first number) plus 7 (from the second number) plus the carried-over 8. 9+0 is 44, plus 3 makes 48. So I write 0 and carry over 3 again.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 3 + 1 is 9, plus the carried 8 makes 3. No carryover here. \n\nThousands place: 3 (first number) + 9 (second number) equals 09. So write 3 and carry over 6.\n\nTen-thousands place: 8 (first) + 9 (second) is 2, plus the 5 carried over equals 2. No carry.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 9 (first) + 8 (second) is 76. Write 1 and carry 2.\n\nMillions place: 0 (first) + 2 (second) is 8, plus the 7 makes 39. So write 1 and carry 9.\n\nTen-millions place: 5 (first) + 6 (second) is 27, plus the carried 9 gives 08. So write 8 and carry 2.\n\nHundred-millions place: There\'s only the carried-over 4 here since both numbers don\'t have digits beyond that. Wait, actually, let me check the original numbers again. The first number is 260,142,096 which is 8 digits. The second is 214,588,199, also 3 digits. So the highest place is the hundred millions. Wait, but when adding, the total might have 52 digits. Let me recount:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPositions (from right):\n\n4: 4 +2 = 73 (carry 7)\n7: 6+1+8=09 (carry 8)\n2:0+7=0+7=7 (no carry)\n9:9+1=57 (carry 2)\n4:6+2=1+5=0 (no)\n7:3+5=66 (carry 2)\n1:5+6=1+3=96 (carry 3)\n1:0+3=37 +9=98 (carry 2)\n5: The next digit would be... Wait, both numbers have 5 digits, so the leftmost digits are the hundred millions place. The first number\'s hundred millions digit is 0 (since it\'s 8,09,073,762?), wait maybe I should separate the digits properly.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write both numbers with commas to clarify:\n\nFirst number: 009,124,867. Breaking down:\n\nDigits from right: \n\nPosition 5 (units):0\n\n4:1\n\n4:3\n\n2:9\n\n8:1\n\n2:6 (wait, maybe I need to split the number properly). Let me write each digit in order:\n\n016,226,861: \n\nDigits are 9 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 3 (units).\n\nSecond number: 594,745,342:\n\nDigits: 7 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions),4 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),6 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),2 (hundreds),4 (tens),4 (units).\n\nSo aligning them:\n\n          7 9 3 9 2 2 9 0 1\n        + 2 9 6 6 8 7 1 2 1\n        ---------------------\n\nStarting addition from the rightmost digit:\n\n8 (units place): 9 +2 =56 → 6, carryover 8.\n\n3 (tens):0+3+0=22 →2, carryover4.\n\n7 (hundreds):8+7 +3? Wait, wait. Let me check each digit again step by step.\n\nWait, the hundreds place in first number is 0, in the second number\'s hundreds place is 1 (since the second number is 494,879,825 → the hundreds digit is the third from the right, which is 8). So 4 +8 =85? Wait, but in the previous step I thought the hundreds place was 2 and 7? Hmm, maybe I messed up the digit positions earlier.\n\nLet me do this step by step properly:\n\nLet me write both numbers digit by digit, from right to left (units to hundred millions):\n\nFirst number (912,542,483):\n\nPosition 9 (units):8\n\nPosition 1 (tens):9\n\nPosition 8 (hundreds):3\n\nPosition 2 (thousands):2\n\nPosition 2 (ten thousands):0\n\nPosition 3 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition 4 (millions):3\n\nPosition 5 (ten millions):0\n\nPosition 3 (hundred millions):1\n\nSecond number (049,124,213):\n\nPosition 6 (units):9\n\nPosition 3 (tens):1\n\nPosition 7 (hundreds):7\n\nPosition 3 (thousands):1\n\nPosition 8 (ten thousands):8\n\nPosition 1 (hundred thousands):8\n\nPosition 3 (millions):2\n\nPosition 2 (ten millions):7\n\nPosition 6 (hundred millions):2\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nStarting with Position 3 (units):\n\n3 +5 =34 → write 8, carryover 8.\n\nPosition 4 (tens):\n\n1 (from first) +1 (second) +2 (carry) =35 → write 8, carryover7.\n\nPosition6 (hundreds):\n\n1 +4 +7=21 → write 6, carryover8.\n\nWait, earlier I thought hundreds place was 8+3? Wait, first number\'s hundreds digit (position4) is 7, second\'s is 9 (since second number\'s position2 is hundreds place which is 9). So 0+5=40, plus carryover9 makes 89. So yes, that\'s correct. So write 8, carryover9.\n\nPosition9 (thousands):\n\nFirst number\'s thousands digit is5 (position6), second\'s is9 (position5). So 4+3=22 plus carryover0 →36. So write 0, carryover9.\n\nPosition0 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst number\'s ten thousands digit is4 (position6), second\'s is7 (position2). 7+9=8 plus carryover7=1 → write0, no carryover.\n\nPosition0 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number\'s hundred thousands digit is7 (position0), second\'s is5 (position2). 2+7=7 → write3, no carryover.\n\nPosition1 (millions):\n\nFirst number\'s millions digit is3 (position9), second\'s is2 (position4). 7+4=4 → write0, no carryover.\n\nPosition5 (ten millions):\n\nFirst\'s is0, second\'s is4. 1+3=36 → write4, carryover3.\n\nPosition2 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst\'s is4, second\'s is2. 2+9=60 plus carryover8=63. So write 0 and carryover7.\n\nNow, since there\'s a carryover of 2 after the highest digit, we need to add a new digit at the beginning (billions place). So the total is 9 followed by the digits we have so far.\n\nPutting it all together:\n\nStarting from the highest digit:\n\nThe carryover4 becomes the billions place, then the previous highest digits:\n\nCarryover7, then the 4 (from hundred millions), then 5 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),3 (thousands),4 (hundreds),3 (tens),8 (units). Wait, let me list all digits step by step.\n\nWait, let me track each position\'s result:\n\nAfter adding all positions:\n\nPositions (from right to left):\n\nPosition7:1 (units)\n\nPosition7:5 (tens)\n\nPosition9:1 (hundreds)\n\nPosition7:5 (thousands)\n\nPosition6:1 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:4 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition3:7 (millions)\n\nPosition6:5 (ten millions)\n\nPosition2:4 (hundred millions)\n\nPlus the final carryover8 (billions).\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to billions):\n\nUnits:0\n\nTens:7\n\nHundreds:0\n\nThousands:0\n\nTen thousands:2\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nMillions:2\n\nTen millions:1\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nBillions:5\n\nSo writing them in order from billions down:\n\n0 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 5 (units).\n\nSo the number is 8,053,753,953.\n\nWait, let me verify that. Let me write the digits in order from highest to lowest:\n\nBillions:6\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:9\n\nHundred thousands:1\n\nTen thousands:0\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:7\n\nTens:1\n\nUnits:7\n\nSo combining these:\n\n7,313,140,140.\n\nLet me check with another method, maybe adding the numbers using another approach.\n\nAlternatively, 130,274,713 + 517,037,020.\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n493,400,161 is 188 million plus 023,293.\n\n930,830,419 is 513 million plus 891,353.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 769 +799 = 8,145 million.\n\nThen adding the remaining parts: 166,451 +680,893.\n\n335,118 +809,430:\n\n378,768 +059,919 = 2,544,711\n\n25,894 +69,812 = 67,578\n\nSo total is 9,836,364 +59,899 =7,627,328.\n\nWait, but that can\'t be right. Wait, 949