es)\n7 (tens)\n2 (hundreds)\n4 (thousands)\n3 (ten thousands)\n3 (hundred thousands)\n2 (millions)\n4 (ten millions)\n3 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, let me confirm that. Let me write each number's digits in order:\n\nFirst number: 3 6 2 5 8 9 4 2 7 (from hundred millions to ones)\nWait, actually, let's do it properly:\n\nLet me list each digit with their place values for both numbers to ensure alignment:\n\nFirst number: 709,447,490\nDigits from left (hundred millions) to right (ones):\n5 (hundred millions)\n5 (ten millions)\n0 (millions)\n2 (hundred thousands)\n4 (ten thousands)\n4 (thousands)\n8 (hundreds)\n1 (tens)\n9 (ones)\n\nSecond number: 830,594,901\nDigits from left (hundred millions) to right (ones):\n4 (hundred millions)\n8 (ten millions)\n4 (millions)\n1 (hundred thousands)\n1 (ten thousands)\n9 (thousands)\n7 (hundreds)\n4 (tens)\n9 (ones)\n\nWait, that might be better. Let me write them vertically aligned properly:\n\nSo when adding, each column should be:\n\nOnes place:\nFirst number's ones digit: 1\nSecond number's ones digit:6\nAdd them: 3+2=24. So 3, carryover 0.\n\nTens place:\nFirst number's tens digit:4\nSecond number's tens digit:9\nPlus carryover 0: 3+4+0=0. So 8, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place:\nFirst number's hundreds digit:1\nSecond number's hundreds digit:7\n7+7=8. So 8, no carryover.\n\nThousands place:\nFirst number's thousands digit:2\nSecond number's thousands digit:4\n4+8=7. Wait, wait, wait, no. Wait the thousands digit for the second number: Looking back, second number's thousands place is 5? Let me check again.\n\nWait the second number is 702,353,531.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:8\n\nTens:2\n\nHundreds:5\n\nThousands:5 (since the number is ...5,613: the thousands digit is 3 in the thousands place?)\n\nWait, perhaps I made an error here. Let me parse the second number again step by step.\n\nThe second number is 974,422,455.\n\nLet me write each digit:\n\nFrom right to left:\n\nPosition 7 (ones):1\n\nPositi