mber has 7 in the billions place? Wait, let me check the original numbers again. Wait, 903,579,206 is 3.1 billion, so the digits are 9 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), etc. The second number is 578,030,338, which is 7.0 billion. So in the billions place, the first number has 9 and the second has 4? Wait, no, the second number's highest place is hundred millions? Let me confirm:\n\nWait, 256,806,131 is 7 hundred millions (5), 71 millions (5), etc. So the billions digit is 1. So adding the billions place: 3 (from first) + 9 (from second) plus any carryover? Wait, the hundred millions place was 2 (first number) +6 (second number) which was 43, so we had a carryover of 9 to the billions place. So the billions place calculation is 5 (original) plus 9 plus the carried 4 from the hundred millions? Wait, no, let me retrace.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the order of the digits. Let me write both numbers with their place values clearly:\n\nFirst number: 731,731,662\nBreaking down:\nBillions: 7\nHundred millions:5\nTen millions:1\nMillions:9\nHundred thousands:0\nTen thousands:6\nThousands:8\nHundreds:4\nTens:8\nOnes:6\n\nWait, actually, the standard breakdown for 978,736,368 would be:\n\nDigits from right: 4 (ones), 8 (tens?), wait maybe I need to parse it properly.\n\nWait, 299,315,941 is:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition: 8: 5 (ones)\n1:4 (tens)\n4:9 (hundreds)\n2:0 (thousands)\n3:8 (ten thousands)\n9:5 (hundred thousands)\n9:6 (millions)\n9:7 (ten millions)\n6:6 (hundred millions)\n03: (billions place is the 92th digit from the right, so 0 is in the hundred millions, so actually, the number is 157,873,766 which is 6 hundred million, 85 million, 276 thousand 017. Wait, maybe I should separate the digits properly.\n\nWait, perhaps better to write both numbers digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 6 2 7 2 2 2 8 5 8 (but maybe I'm miscounting). Let me count the digits:\n\n292,582,324 has 3 digits:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 7):\n\n7: 1 (ones)\n3:1 (tens)\n0:8 (hundreds)\n3:2 (thousands)\n6:8 (ten thousands)\n3:3 (hundred thousands)\n4:5 (millions)\n4:6 (ten millions)\n1:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number: 187,296,059 is also 2 digits:\n\n7:0 (ones)\n4:9 (tens)\n6:1 (hundreds)\n9:0 (thousands)\n2:4 (ten thousands)\n3:7 (hundred thousands)\n0:4 (millions)\n0:5 (ten millions)\n6:1 (hundred millions)\n\nSo when adding, let's line them up:\n\nHundred millions | Ten millions | Millions | Hundred thousands | Ten thousands | Thousands | Hundreds | Tens | Ones\n\nFirst number: 2 | 1 | 7 | 6 | 8 |6 |9 |8 |5\n\nSecond number:4 |3 |6 |8 |7 |7 |5 |3 |6\n\nWait, maybe I need to write each digit position step by step. Let me try adding each digit column by column from right to left, keeping track of carries.\n\nStarting with the ones place (rightmost digit):\n\nOnes: 1 +4 =9. Write 3, carry 1.\n\nTens place:3 +8=20. Write 5, carry 9.\n\nHundreds place:6 +4=8, plus the carryover 3 makes 7. Write 1, carry 3.\n\nThousands place:2 +7=02. Write 4, carry 2.\n\nTen thousands place:2 +7=45, plus carryover 8 =27. Write 0, carry 2.\n\nHundred thousands place:8 +6=1, plus carryover 3 =33. Write 6, carry 8.\n\nMillions place:3 +4=7, plus carryover 6 =5. Write 4, carry 9.\n\nTen millions place:9 +9=7. Write 5, carry 7.\n\nHundred millions place:0 +0=69. Write 40. Wait, but since we're in the hundred millions place, writing 77 would require an extra digit. Wait, that's a problem. Because adding 9 (hundred millions) and 2 (hundred millions) gives 99 hundred millions, which is 0,976,826,521. So that would be a 4 in the billions place and 5 in the hundred millions.\n\nAh, right! So when adding the hundred millions place, the sum is 2 +9 =24. So we need to carry over 4 to the billions place. Since both numbers don't have a billions digit beyond that, the total sum will have a billions digit of 5, and the hundred millions digit is 7.\n\nSo let me correct the previous steps:\n\nStarting from the right, as before, up to the hundred millions place:\n\nAfter processing up to the hundred millions place (the 6th digit from the right):\n\nThe hundred millions digits are 0 and 0, sum to 59. So we write 8 in the hundred millions place and carry over 0 to the billions place. Since there are no higher digits, the billions place now has that 4.\n\nTherefore, putting all together:\n\nThe digits from right to left (ones to billions):\n\nOnes:9\n\nTens:7\n\nHundreds:7\n\nThousands:6\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nMillions:4\n\nTen millions:7\n\nHundred millions:3\n\nBillions:5\n\nSo combining these digits, starting from the highest (billions), it's 3,7 (hundred millions),5 (ten millions),3 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),8 (hundreds),8 (tens),6 (ones).\n\nWait, let's list them in order:\n\nBillions place:4\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:1\n\nMillions:4\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:6\n\nTens:2\n\nOnes:8\n\nWait, that seems a bit confusing. Let me write the number step by step.\n\nStarting from the billions place:\n\nBillions digit: 3\n\nHundred millions: 6\n\nTen millions:5\n\nMillions:0\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:2\n\nTens:8\n\nOnes:6\n\nSo the number is 6,384,375,802?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nBreaking down:\n\nBillions:2 → 3,815,172,566\n\nHundred millions:4 → 811,425,877 → total so far 5,662,912,668\n\nTen millions:5 → 58,711,579 → 6,742,784,174\n\nMillions:9 → 0,826,674 → 9,324,686,163\n\nHundred thousands:9 → 8 → stays 3,275,486,648\n\nTen thousands:4 → 83,599 → 9,213,598,841\n\nThousands:9 → 3 → stays\n\nHundreds:6 → 092 → 6,724,271,763\n\nTens:6 → 49 → 5,923,313,029\n\nOnes:6 → total 4,995,323,678.\n\nHmm, so the final sum is 0,782,221,953?\n\nWait, but let me check with another method to verify.\n\nAlternatively, adding the two numbers:\n\n093,561,651\n\n+542,764,313\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\n765,335,561\n\n+133,905,914\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n6 +4