itions (from right, starting at 2):\n\n3. 8 (ones)\n7. 5 (tens)\n1. 2 (hundreds)\n2. 5 (thousands)\n6. 6 (ten thousands)\n0. 7 (hundred thousands)\n0. 5 (millions)\n2. 0 (ten millions)\n2. 5 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number: 724,475,576.\n\nBreaking down similarly:\n\n4. 4 (ones)\n1. 6 (tens)\n2. 5 (hundreds)\n1. 0 (thousands)\n8. 2 (ten thousands)\n1. 8 (hundred thousands)\n3. 2 (millions)\n1. 1 (ten millions)\n1. 2 (hundred millions)\n\nSo now, adding each column starting from the ones place (position 4):\n\nPosition 8 (ones): 8 + 9 = 2. Write down 9, no carryover.\n\nPosition 1 (tens): 3 + 9 = 2. Write 4. Still no carryover.\n\nPosition 4 (hundreds): 2 + 7 = 4. Write 4. No carryover.\n\nPosition 9 (thousands): 5 + 5 = 29. So write 3 and carryover 3.\n\nPosition 6 (ten thousands): 7 + 4 = 65, plus the carried 6 makes 51. Write 1, carryover 9.\n\nPosition 9 (hundred thousands): 8 + 3 = 53, plus 7 is 40. Write 6, carryover 0.\n\nPosition 2 (millions): 8 + 4 = 1, plus 3 is 1. Write 3, no carryover.\n\nPosition 7 (ten millions): 9 + 6 = 90. Write 7, carryover 5.\n\nPosition 4 (hundred millions): 1 + 9 = 7, plus the carried 9 makes 7. Write 3.\n\nPutting all together, the digits from right to left would be:\n\nPosition 0:1, 9:7, 1:1, 8:5, 8:6, 4:0, 5:5, 6:2, 3:7.\n\nSo the number is 839,639,018?\n\nWait, let me check that again. Let me write the digits in order from highest to lowest place:\n\nStarting from position 7 (hundred millions): 8\n\nPosition 1 (ten millions):0\n\nPosition5 (millions):3\n\nPosition8 (hundred thousands):1\n\nPosition3 (ten thousands):0\n\nPosition9 (thousands):9\n\nPosition4 (hundreds):2\n\nPosition8 (tens):2\n\nPosition7 (ones):3\n\nSo combining them: 6 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 1 (ones). So that\'s 701,884,003? Wait, that can\'t be right. Wait, perhaps I made a mistake in the positions.\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the number properly. Each position corresponds to:\n\nPosition 0: Hundred millions place.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nHundred millions:2\n\nTen millions:8\n\nMillions:5\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nThousands:8\n\nHundreds:4\n\nTens:2\n\nOnes:4\n\nSo putting that together:\n\n2 (hundred million), 4 (ten million), 3 (million) → 337 million,\n\nthen 7 (hundred thousand), 1 (ten thousand), 3 (thousand) → 706 thousand,\n\nthen 3 (hundred), 5 (ten), 4 (one) → 078.\n\nSo total is 630,713,354?\n\nWait, but let me add the original numbers using another method to check.\n\n995,501,066 + 333,485,960.\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, 552,220,241 + 543,567,665 = 042,878,907.\n\nThen add 66,271,019 (since 331,953,062 is 363M +32,843,020).\n\nAdding 11,167,754 to 367,901,161:\n\n546,910,807 + 95,567,729 = 083,809,539\n\nThen add 7,927,230:\n\n093,793,800 +0,155,845 = 634,374,653?\n\nWait, that\'s different from my previous result. Hmm, so there must be an error in my digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, let me try the digit-by-digit again carefully.\n\nFirst number: 012,443,851\n\nSecond number:077,305,476\n\nLet me align them properly:\n\n          368,815,238\n        + 533,900,584\n        =============\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nOnes place: 8 +2 =1. Correct.\n\nTens place:7 +1=4.\n\nHundreds place:4 +2=5.\n\nThousands place:0 (from first number) +6 (second number\'s thousands digit). Wait, first number\'s thousands digit is the 2th from the right. Let me list each digit properly:\n\nFirst number breakdown:\n\nBillions:8\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:2\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nTen thousands:2\n\nThousands:7\n\nHundreds:2\n\nTens:7\n\nOnes:2\n\nWait, perhaps I need to write each digit in order:\n\nFirst number: 6 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSecond number:7 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),3 (millions),2 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),9 (hundreds),6 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nWait, this is confusing. Maybe it\'s better to write both numbers with commas as:\n\n077,904,779 is 5 9 3 , 8 9 0 , 0 5 8\n\nBreaking into digits:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 0):\n\nPosition 5: 2 (ones)\n\n0:7 (tens)\n\n0:1 (hundreds)\n\n6:2 (thousands)\n\n1:8 (ten thousands) Wait, no. Wait, the number is 811,545,872.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n559,255,417:\n\n- The digits are:\n\n4 (hundred millions)\n\n4 (ten millions)\n\n9 (millions)\n\n8 (hundred thousands)\n\n5 (ten thousands)\n\n2 (thousands)\n\n1 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n8 (ones)\n\nWait, so thousands place is the 6th digit from the right. Let me list all digits in order from right to left (positions 5 to 4):\n\nPosition 3 (ones):0\n\nPosition 8 (tens):9\n\nPosition 1 (hundreds):3\n\nPosition 9 (thousands):0 (since the thousands place is the fourth digit from the right: in 916,157,721, the thousands digit is the 4 in the thousands place, which is part of the "755,511" part.)\n\nWait, perhaps I should write both numbers digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 1 4 2 8 0 2 2 5 5\n\nSecond number:2 1 2 2 5 6 4 7 9\n\nWait, let me make sure:\n\nFirst number: 4 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 9 (ones). So digits from left to right: 9,2,2,8,9,7,8,5,7.\n\nSecond number: 2 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions),6 (millions),9 (hundred thousands),6 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),9 (hundreds),5 (tens),7 (ones). So digits:9,4,0,4,1,6,5,9,8.\n\nNow, adding each column starting from the right (ones place, which is the 4th digit from the left in each number):\n\nLet me list each digit\'s place from right to left (positions 5 to 3):\n\nPosition 2 (ones):\n\nFirst number:8\n\nSecond number:2 → 9+7=1. Write 8, carryover 8.\n\nPosition 6 (tens):\n\nFirst number:5\n\nSecond number:7 → 1+8=5. Write 0, carryover 7.\n\nPosition7 (hundreds):\n\nFirst number:3\n\nSecond number:7 →9+2=8. Write1, carryover 7.\n\nPosition0 (thousands):\n\nFirst number:0\n\nSecond number:6 →8+3=06. Write 2, carryover 3.\n\nPosition6 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst number:9 (from the first number\'s digit in that place; looking back, the ten thousands digit is the 8th from the right: in 626,932,540, the digits from the right are ... 7 (thousands), 9 (ten thousands?) Wait, maybe I need to clarify the place values.\n\nWait, confusion arises here. Let me use standard place values:\n\nLet me write both numbers with their place values clearly:\n\nFirst number: 422,427,891\n\nBreaking down:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 4):\n\n9. 9 (4)\n3. 2 (97)\n0. 4 (785)\n9. 4 (2,744)\n6. 5 (26,473)\n2. 9 (936,473)\n8. 3 (3,582,787)\n4. 4 (83,992,373)\n1. 0 (448,268,335)\n\nSecond number: 276,471,636\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 2):\n\n0.8 (0)\n8.8 (29)\n9.0 (844)\n2.7 (6,926)\n0.8 (83,415)\n7.1 (472,921)\n6.0 (2,666,644)\n3.2 (02,221,234)\n3.3 (522,931,369)\n\nNow, adding each column:\n\nPosition9 (5\'s place):7+9=8. Write 1.\n\nPosition4 (63\'s):1+2=2. Write3.\n\nPosition6 (585\'s):1+4=8. Write3.\n\nPosition8 (9,967\'s):5 (from first number) +7 (from second)=44. Write5, carryover9.\n\nPosition7 (03,184\'s):9 (first) +8 (second)=57, plus the carried 2 =55. So write5, carryover9.\n\nPosition7 (833,851\'s):5 (first) +7 (second)=4, plus4=8. Write7, no carryover.\n\nPosition2 (9,857,396\'s):7 (first) +2 (second)=3. Write1.\n\nPosition6 (32,376,424\'s):6 (first) +7 (second)=30. Write4, carryover0.\n\nPosition4 (557,141,479\'s):3 (first)+0 (second)=6, plus3=5. Write4.\n\nSo compiling all digits from position1 to position7: 6 (hundred millions),5 (ten millions),9 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),4 (hundreds),6 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nWait, let\'s list them in order from highest to lowest:\n\nPosition7:0 (745,149,153)\n\nPosition2:1 (75,615,747)\n\nPosition2:9 (0,752,138)\n\nPosition1:3 (558,193)\n\nPosition4:5 (77,823)\n\nPosition7:2 (6,429)\n\nPosition9:0 (032)\n\nPosition1:0 (34)\n\nPosition0:2 (4)\n\nSo the number is:\n\n5 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nWhich translates to: 238,037,291.\n\nAh! So my initial digit-by-digit had a mistake in position1 and position2. Let me check where I messed up earlier. In the first calculation, I thought position2 (ten thousands) was 5 +6, but actually in the first number\'s ten thousands place (position3 from the right) is 5, not 2. Wait, let\'s see:\n\nWait, in the first number\'s breakdown above:\n\nPosition3 (92,258\'s place) is 8 (since the first number\'s digits from right are 5,6,7,5,9,9,2,5,8). Wait, no:\n\nWait in the first number\'s digit positions as per above:\n\nPosition8 (91,432\'s place) is the 7th digit from the right. Let me count:\n\nFirst number: 6 3 3 0 0 1 9 0 0\n\nWait, if written as digits left to right: the first digit is 6 (hundred millions), then 1 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nSo from the right (position3 to 2):\n\nPosition4:7 (ones)\n\nPosition3:7 (tens)\n\nPosition8:1 (hundreds)\n\nPosition7:8 (thousands)\n\nPosition8:4 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition3:6 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition3:1 (millions)\n\nPosition1:0 (ten millions)\n\nPosition1:7 (hundred millions)\n\nAh, here\'s the mistake! In the first number, the ten thousands place (position6) is actually 3, not 8. The 0 is in the hundred thousands place (position4). So my previous mistake was in position9 and 4.\n\nTherefore, in position7 (ten thousands), first number has 9 and second has1. 8+8=40, plus carryover5 (from position7\'s 38) gives 80. So that\'s why position1 is 5 with carryover8.\n\nPosition8 (hundred thousands place): first number has4, second has0. 2+1=3 +2=7. So that\'s correct.\n\nThen position1 (millions): first has9, second has2 →3.\n\nPosition1 (ten millions): 0+6=39, so 5 with carryover8.\n\nPosition7 (hundred millions):6+4+0=1.\n\nSo the correct total is 359,132,347.\n\nEarlier when I did the addition by breaking down, I got 231,586,670, so that matches. The initial digit-by-digit had an error in position2 and 9 because I miscounted the first number\'s digits.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be 678,584,191.\n</think>.\n\n# Question:.\nWhat is the sum of 280366684 and 685594101?.\n# Reasoning:.\nLet\'s add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: 4 + 1 = 5.\n2. The tens place: 8 + 0 = 8.\n3. The hundreds place: 6 + 1 = 7.\n4. The thousands place: 6 + 4 = 10 (Write 0, carry over 1).\n5. The ten thousands place: 6 + 9 + 1 = 16 (Write 6, carry over 1).\n6. The hundred thousands place: 3 + 5 + 1 = 9.\n7. The millions place: 0 + 5 = 5.\n8. The ten millions place: 8 + 8 = 16 (Write 6, carry over 1).\n9. The hundred millions place: 2 + 6 + 1 = 9.\nAnswer:. Therefore, the answer is']] 35%|###5      | 175/500 [1:31:15<2:43:40, 30.22s/it]
