    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": " 1,281,637,515. Wait, I think I might have miscalculated in the process. Let me verify using another method.\n\nAlternatively, adding 990061514 and 397334501:\n\nBreak down each number into parts:\n\n990,061,514 can be written as 900,000,000 + 90,000,000 + 61,514.\n\n397,334,501 is 300,000,000 + 97,000,000 + 334,501.\n\nAdding the large chunks first:\n\n900,000,000 + 300,000,000 = 1,200,000,000.\n\n90,000,000 + 97,000,000 = 187,000,000.\n\n61,514 + 334,501 = 396,015.\n\nNow add all these sums together:\n\n1,200,000,000 + 187,000,000 = 1,387,000,000.\n\n1,387,000,000 + 396,015 = 1,387,396,015.\n\nWait a second, this contradicts my previous result. Clearly, I made a mistake in my initial digit-by-digit addition. Let me figure out where I went wrong.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nNumber1: 990,061,514\n\nNumber2: 397,334,501\n\nLet's add them properly step by step:\n\nAligning the numbers:\n\n```\n  990,061,514\n+ 397,334,501\n--------------\n```\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\nOnes: 4 + 1 = 5 \u2192 write down 5.\n\nTens: 1 + 0 = 1 \u2192 write down 1.\n\nHundreds: 5 + 4 = 9 \u2192 write down 9.\n\nThousands: 1 + 5 = 6 \u2192 write down 6.\n\nTen thousands: 6 + 3 = 9 \u2192 write down 9.\n\nHundred thousands: 0 + 3 = 3 \u2192 write down 3.\n\nMillions: 9 + 7 = 16 \u2192 write down 6 and carry over 1.\n\nTen millions: 9 + 9 + 1 (carry) = 19 \u2192 write down 9 and carry over 1.\n\nHundred millions: 9 (from first number) + 3 (from second) + 1 (carry) = 13 \u2192 write down 3 and carry over 1.\n\nBillions: The first number has 9 in the billions place, the second has 3. Adding them plus carryover 1: 9 + 3 + 1 = 13 \u2192 write down 3 and carry over 1.\n\nSince there are no more digits, the final carryover 1 becomes the next digit (trillions place).\n\nPutting it all together, the sum is:\n\n1 (trillion) 3 (hundred billions), 3 (ten billions), 9 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nWait, let me structure it properly:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:5\n\nTens:1\n\nHundreds:9\n\nThousands:6\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nMillions:6 (with carryover 1)\n\nTen millions:9 (with carryover 1)\n\nHundred millions:3 (with carryover 1)\n\nBillions:3 (from 9+3+1) \u2192 actually, wait:\n\nWait, let's track each digit:\n\nThe digits from right to left:\n\nPosition 1 (Ones): 4+1=5\n\nPosition 2 (Tens):1+0=1\n\nPosition3 (Hundreds):5+4=9\n\nPosition4 (Thousands):1+5=6\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):6+3=9\n\nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):0+3=3\n\nPosition7 (Millions):9 (from first number's millions) + 7 (from second's millions) = 16 \u2192 write 6, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (Ten millions):9 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carry)=19 \u2192 write9, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (Hundred millions):9 (first) +3 (second) +1 (carry)=13 \u2192 write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition10 (Billions):9 (first) +3 (second) +1 (carry)=13 \u2192 write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition11 (Trillions): carryover1.\n\nSo the final digits are:\n\n1 (trillion), 3 (hundred billion), 3 (ten billion), 9 (billion), 3 (hundred million), 9 (ten million), 6 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 6 (thousand), 9 (hundred), 1 (ten), 5 (one).\n\nWait, putting this into standard form:\n\nTrillions:1\n\nHundred billions:3\n\nTen billions:3\n\nBillions:9\n\nHundred millions:3\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:6\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:9\n\nTens:1\n\nOnes:5\n\nSo the number is 1,339,396,396,915? Wait that can't be right. Wait, maybe I miscounted the positions.\n\nWait, let's list the digits from the highest place:\n\nStarting with the trillions digit (position11): 1\n\nThen hundred billions (position10):3\n\nTen billions (position9):3\n\nBillions (position8):9\n\nHundred millions (position7):3\n\nTen millions (position6):9\n\nMillions (position5):6\n\nHundred thousands (position4):3\n\nTen thousands (position3):9\n\nThousands (position2):6\n\nHundreds (position1):9\n\nTens (position0):1\n\nWait, this seems off. Let's write it digit by digit:\n\nTrillions:1\n\nBillions place: 3 (hundred billions) + 3 (ten billions) +9 (billions) \u2192 but actually each digit is separate.\n\nWait, the digits are:\n\n1 (trillion), 3 (hundred billion), 3 (ten billion), 9 (billion), 3 (hundred million), 9 (ten million), 6 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 6 (thousand), 9 (hundred), 1 (ten), 5 (one).\n\nSo the number is 1,339,396,396,915? Wait that seems too large. But 990 million plus 397 million is about 1.387 billion, so the correct sum should be around 1,387,396,015. Where did I go wrong?\n\nAh! I see the mistake. In the billions place:\n\nWait, the first number is 990,061,514 which is 0.990 billion, so its billions digit is 0 (since it's 990 million, which is less than a billion). The second number is 397,334,501 which is 0.397 billion. So both are less than a billion. Therefore, when adding, the total should be less than 1.4 billion, not over a trillion. So my earlier digit-by-digit approach was wrong because I miscounted the number of digits.\n\nLet me try again. Let's write the numbers properly with commas to see their actual place values.\n\nFirst number: 990,061,514\n\nThis is 9 hundred millions (9), 9 ten millions (9), 0 millions (0), 0 hundred thousands (0), 6 ten thousands (6), 1 thousand (1), 5 hundred (5), 1 ten (1), 4 ones (4).\n\nWait, breaking it down:\n\nDigits from right:\n\nPosition1:4 (ones)\n\nPosition2:1 (tens)\n\nPosition3:5 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:1 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:0 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:9 (millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition10:0 (billions place, since it's a 9-digit number).\n\nSecond number:397,334,501\n\nDigits from right:\n\nPosition1:1 (ones)\n\nPosition2:0 (tens)\n\nPosition3:5 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:4 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:3 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:7 (millions)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:3 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition10:0 (billions).\n\nAdding them:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:4+1=5\n\nTens:1+0=1\n\nHundreds:5+5=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover1.\n\nThousands:1+4 +1=6 \u2192 write6, carryover0.\n\nTen thousands:6+3=9.\n\nHundred thousands:0+3=3.\n\nMillions:9+7=16 \u2192 write6, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:9+9 +1=19 \u2192 write9, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:9+3 +1=13 \u2192 write3, carryover1.\n\nBillions:0+0 +1=1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n5 (ones),1 (tens),0 (hundreds),6 (thousands),9 (ten thousands),3 (hundred thousands),6 (millions),9 (ten millions),3 (hundred millions),1 (billions).\n\nArranged from billions down: 1,3 (hundred millions),9 (ten millions),6 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),0 (hundreds),1 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 1,396,396,015.\n\nWait, let me write this with commas:\n\n1 (billion), 396 (million), 396 (thousand), 015.\n\nWait, 1,396,396,015? Let's check with the previous method:\n\n990,061,514 + 397,334,501.\n\nAdding the millions:\n\n990,000,000 + 397,000,000 = 1,387,000,000.\n\nAdding the remaining parts:\n\n61,514 + 334,501 = 396,015.\n\nTotal sum: 1,387,000,000 + 396,015 = 1,387,396,015.\n\nAh! There's a discrepancy here. The digit-by-digit addition gave 1,396,396,015 but the other method gives 1,387,396,015. So I must have made an error in the digit addition.\n\nLet's redo the digit-by-digit step carefully.\n\nFirst number: 990,061,514 \u2192 digits from right:\n\nPosition1:4\n\nPosition2:1\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition4:1\n\nPosition5:6\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition7:9\n\nPosition8:9\n\nPosition9:9\n\nPosition10:0 (since it's a 9-digit number).\n\nSecond number: 397,334,501 \u2192 digits from right:\n\nPosition1:1\n\nPosition2:0\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition4:4\n\nPosition5:3\n\nPosition6:3\n\nPosition7:7\n\nPosition8:9\n\nPosition9:3\n\nPosition10:0.\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1 (Ones):4+1=5 \u21925\n\nPosition2 (Tens):1+0=1 \u21921\n\nPosition3 (Hundreds):5+5=10 \u21920 with carryover1\n\nPosition4 (Thousands):1+4 +1(carry)=6 \u21926, carryover0\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):6+3=9 \u21929\n\nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):0+3=3 \u21923\n\nPosition7 (Millions):9+7=16 \u21926, carryover1\n\nPosition8 (Ten millions):9+9 +1=19 \u21929, carryover1\n\nPosition9 (Hundred millions):9+3 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nPosition10 (Billions):0+0 +1=1 \u21921.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition10 (Billions):1\n\nPosition9 (Hundred millions):3\n\nPosition8 (Ten millions):9\n\nPosition7 (Millions):6\n\nPosition6 (Hundred thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (Ten thousands):9\n\nPosition4 (Thousands):6\n\nPosition3 (Hundreds):0\n\nPosition2 (Tens):1\n\nPosition1 (Ones):5\n\nSo the number is 1 (billion), 3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 5 (ones). \n\nPutting commas:\n\n1,396,396,015. But according to the other method, it should be 1,387,396,015. Where's the mistake?\n\nAh! Wait, in the millions place:\n\nPosition7 (Millions): first number has 9 (millions digit) and second has 7 (millions digit). So 9+7=16. That's correct, so millions digit is 6, carryover1 to the ten millions.\n\nPosition8 (Ten millions): first has 9, second has9. So 9+9 +1 (carry)=19 \u21929, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (Hundred millions): first has9, second has3. 9+3 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition10 (Billions): 0+0 +1=1.\n\nThus the total is indeed 1,396,396,015.\n\nWait but when adding the whole numbers:\n\n990,061,514 + 397,334,501.\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 990,061,514\n\nAdd 397,334,501:\n\nAdd 300,000,000 \u2192 1,290,061,514\n\nAdd 97,000,000 \u2192 1,387,061,514\n\nAdd 334,501 \u2192 1,387,396,015.\n\nWait, this contradicts the digit-by-digit result. There's a mistake here. Where?\n\nWait, the first number is 990,061,514