lion), 3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),0 (hundreds),1 (tens),5 (ones).\n\nWhich is 1,396,396,015.\n\nBut when adding the entire numbers:\n\n990,061,514 + 397,334,501.\n\nLet me compute this numerically:\n\n990,061,514\n\n+397,334,501\n\n= (990,000,000 + 397,000,000) + (61,514 + 334,501)\n\n= 1,387,000,000 + 396,015 = 1,387,396,015.\n\nWait a minute, there's a contradiction here. Where is the error?\n\nAh! I realize now that the first number is 990,061,514. Breaking down:\n\n- 990,000,000 (990 million)\n\n- 61,514\n\nSo adding to 397,334,501 (which is 397 million plus 334,501):\n\nTotal is 1,387 million plus 396,015, which is 1,387,396,015.\n\nBut the digit-by-digit method gave 1,396,396,015. The difference is 9 million. That suggests a digit placement error in the ten millions place.\n\nWait, let's re-express the first number as 990,061,514:\n\n- Hundred millions:9\n\n- Ten millions:9\n\n- Millions:0 (since it's 990 million, so after hundred millions (9), ten millions (9), the millions place is 0?)\n\nWait, perhaps I miscounted the digits. Let me write the number 990,061,514 with commas:\n\nIt's 990,061,514 \u2192 990 (million), 061 (thousand), 514. So the breakdown:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions)\n\n- 9 (ten millions)\n\n- 0 (millions)\n\n- 0 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 6 (ten thousands)\n\n- 1 (thousands)\n\n- 5 (hundreds)\n\n- 1 (tens)\n\n- 4 (ones).\n\nAh! Here's the mistake! In the first number, the millions place is 0, not 9. The first number is 990 million, which is:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions)\n\n- 9 (ten millions)\n\n- 0 (millions)\n\nSo the millions digit (position7) is 0, not9. That was my error in the digit-by-digit addition earlier.\n\nSo correcting that:\n\nFirst number's digits:\n\nPosition1:4\n\nPosition2:1\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition4:1\n\nPosition5:6\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition7:0 (millions place)\n\nPosition8:9 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition10:0 (billion