e number is 4,510,146,463.\n\nBut when adding the millions parts, I had 754 +270 =7,500 million. Hmm. Wait, where is the discrepancy?\n\nAh, I see the problem in the millions calculation. Wait the first number is 367,028,801 which is 727 million plus 920,762. The second is443,337,677 which is226 million plus75,501. So total millions:761+037 = 2,933 million. Then the remaining parts (the 208,486 and 51,989) sum to 201,741. So total is 4,731,646,742 + 195,213 = 3,078,316,615. But according to digit-by-digit, it\'s3,411,469,169. \n\nSo there must be an error in one of the methods.\n\nWait let me do the digit-by-digit again:\n\nWait, position1 is millions place. \n\nFirst number\'s millions digit is1 (since the number is 162,795,362, the millions digit is the 9 in 362, so yes, the seventh digit from the right (since the number is 3 digits). Let me see:\n\nFirst number\'s digits (right to left):\n\nPositions 8-7:\n\n0:7,3:3,9:1,8:0,2:2,8:9,8:2,6:2,2:3.\n\nSo position4 is the millions digit (4), and position6 is ten millions (7), position5 hundred millions (2).\n\nSecond number\'s digits:\n\nPosition9 (millions):1 (from 402,324,536: the millions digit is 1 in 594, so yes).\n\nSo adding position1 (millions):1+7=1. So the millions digit is2. That\'s correct.\n\nPosition5 (ten millions):7 (first) +0 (second) =21 → so 3 in ten millions, carryover0.\n\nPosition3 (hundred millions):4+6=61 plus the carryover2 →84 →5 in hundred millions, carryover0.\n\nThus, the hundred millions is6, the carryover gives the 3 in billions.\n\nThus, the total is 8,114,663,184.\n\nWait then why does the other method give a different result?\n\nAh, because when I broke it into millions, I considered the first number\'s millions as 697 million, but actually the breakdown is:\n\nFirst number: 589,740,379 is 124 million plus 588,109. The second number:977,220,246 is796 million plus70,590.\n\nAdding the millions: 384 +334 =7,957 million. Then adding the remaining parts (112,660 +66,509 =911,048). So tota