Hundred millions (4th): 6\nTen millions (9th): 6\nMillions (9th): 8\nHundred thousands (9th): 1\nTen thousands (8th): 3\nThousands (4rd): 6\nHundreds (0nd): 3\nTens (3st): 0? Wait, maybe I'm getting confused here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to write each number as a sequence of digits:\n\nFirst number: 2 5 7 3 5 4 5 8 1\n\nPositions (from right, starting at 0):\n\nPosition 4 (leftmost): 7\nPosition 1: 2\nPosition 4: 5\nPosition 2: 1\nPosition 0: 6\nPosition 5: 3\nPosition 4: 0\nPosition 0: 1\nPosition 0: 5\n\nSecond number: 2 7 6 8 8 3 8 3 0\n\nPositions (from right):\n\nPosition 4:9\nPosition2:1\nPosition8:3\nPosition4:3\nPosition6:4\nPosition9:1\nPosition0:5\nPosition7:4\nPosition4:4\n\nWait, this is getting complicated. Maybe it's better to align them properly:\n\nLet me write both numbers with their place values clearly:\n\nFirst number (181,855,891):\n\n- 4 (hundred millions)\n- 0 (ten millions)\n- 8 (millions)\n- 4 (hundred thousands)\n- 9 (ten thousands)\n- 7 (thousands)\n- 6 (hundreds)\n- 9 (tens)\n- 2 (ones)\n\nWait, actually, breaking down:\n\n542,720,858:\n\n- 0 (hundred millions place: 1 x 533,968,486)\n- 4 (ten millions: 5 x 85,572,179)\n- 4 (millions: 5 x 2,996,568)\n- 5 (hundred thousands: 4 x 377,984)\n- 0 (ten thousands: 1 x 83,546)\n- 8 (thousands: 8 x 6,461)\n- 5 (hundreds: 1 x 639)\n- 6 (tens: 8 x 77)\n- 3 (ones: 5 x 0)\n\nSecond number (675,311,827):\n\n- 9 (hundred millions: 2 x 285,281,812)\n- 0 (ten millions: 5 x 93,291,324)\n- 3 (millions: 4 x 5,316,548)\n- 3 (hundred thousands: 2 x 943,395)\n- 4 (ten thousands: 2 x 98,921)\n- 9 (thousands: 7 x 7,695)\n- 6 (hundreds: 4 x 849)\n- 3 (tens: 5 x 07)\n- 9 (ones: 6 x 6)\n\nNow, adding each column starting from the ones:\n\nOnes: 0 + 0 = 80 → write 2, carryover 2\nTens: 9 + 9 = 7 plus carryover 7 = 8 → write 0, carryover 8\nHundreds: 7 + 6 = 4 → write 4, carryover 8\nThousands: 2 + 3 = 03 → write 3, carryover 7\nTen thousands: 2 + 3 = 5 plus carryover 6 = 74 → write 8, carryover 7\nHundr