ds:0+9=9  \nTen thousands:9+0=9  \nHundred thousands:9+1=10 \u21920, carryover1  \nMillions:0+5 +1=6  \nTen millions:8+9=17 \u21927, carryover1  \nHundred millions:9+8+1=18 \u21928, carryover1  \nBillions: carryover1 \u21921  \n\nSo writing all digits:  \nBillions:1,  \nHundred millions:8,  \nTen millions:7,  \nMillions:6,  \nHundred thousands:0,  \nTen thousands:9,  \nThousands:9,  \nHundreds:5,  \nTens:2,  \nUnits:3  \n\nThus, the number is 1,876,099,523. Wait, but when combining the digits, the hundred thousands place is 0, ten thousands is 9, thousands is 9, so the number is:\n\n1 (billion), 8 (hundred million), 7 (ten million), 6 (million), 0 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 9 (thousand), 5 (hundred), 2 (ten), 3 (unit). \n\nSo written as 1,876,099,523. But my previous answer had 1,876,100,523. There's a mistake here. Let's check the hundred thousands place again.\n\nWait, the hundred thousands digit comes from the 6th digit from the right:\n\nFirst number: 980,990,038  \nBreakdown:  \nPositions (right to left):  \n1:8 (units)  \n2:3 (tens)  \n3:0 (hundreds)  \n4:0 (thousands)  \n5:9 (ten thousands)  \n6:9 (hundred thousands)  \n7:0 (millions)  \n8:8 (ten millions)  \n9:9 (hundred millions)  \n\nSecond number:895,109,485  \nBreakdown:  \n1:5 (units)  \n2:8 (tens)  \n3:4 (hundreds)  \n4:9 (thousands)  \n5:0 (ten thousands)  \n6:1 (hundred thousands)  \n7:5 (millions)  \n8:9 (ten millions)  \n9:8 (hundred millions)  \n\nAdding the hundred thousands place (position6):  \n9 (first number) +1 (second number) =10 \u21920 with carryover1. So the hundred thousands digit is 0, and carryover to millions place.\n\nThe ten thousands place (position5):  \n9 (first) +0 (second) =9. So that's correct.\n\nThousands place (position4):  \n0 (first) +9 (second)=9.\n\nSo the number is:  \n1 (billions),  \n8 (hundred millions),  \n7 (ten millions),  \n6 (millions),  \n0 (hundred thousands),  \n9 (ten thousands),  \n9 (thousands),  \n5 (hundreds),  \n2 (tens),  \n3 (units). \n\n