en thousands):6+7=1? Wait wait, hold on, maybe I messed up the digit positions here. Let me parse each number digit by digit properly.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write each number's digits in order. Let's take the first number: 405,404,747. Breaking down each digit from right to left:\n\nDigits of 073102466:\nPosition 5 (ones):5\nPosition0:2\nPosition3:7\nPosition7:2\nPosition7:8 (the fifth digit from the right)\nWait, maybe I'm getting confused here. Let's count properly:\n\nThe number is 502,878,620. Let's remove commas: 3 9 5 3 4 3 8 8 2. Wait, that's 5 digits. Let's count positions from right (units) as position 3:\n\nStarting from the right:\nPosition6:6 (units)\nPosition2:1 (tens)\nPosition6:7 (hundreds)\nPosition7:4 (thousands)\nPosition3:3 (ten thousands)\nPosition9:3 (hundred thousands)\nPosition5:3 (millions)\nPosition3:0 (ten millions)\nPosition7:1 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number: 318,645,196 → 8 digits as well:\nDigits: 6 8 1 6 5 0 8 8 2\n\nBreaking down:\nPosition7:1 (units)\nPosition7:4 (tens)\nPosition9:9 (hundreds)\nPosition0:2 (thousands)\nPosition5:1 (ten thousands)\nPosition1:2 (hundred thousands)\nPosition7:6 (millions)\nPosition8:4 (ten millions)\nPosition1:4 (hundred millions)\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nStarting with Position2 (units):\n8 +3 =65 → 6, carryover 0\n\nPosition0 (tens):\n3 (from first number) +0 (second number) =44, plus carryover 8 →39 →1, carryover2\n\nPosition0 (hundreds):\n7 (first) +0 (second)=1 +2 →9, carryover7\n\nPosition6 (thousands):\n4 (first) +7 (second)=6 →7, carryover9\n\nPosition3 (ten thousands):\n9 (first) +7 (second)=5 →1, carryover8\n\nPosition2 (hundred thousands):\n7 (first) +6 (second)=4 →7, carryover4\n\nPosition2 (millions):\n4 (first) +2 (second)=32 →7, carryover6\n\nPosition1 (ten millions):\n3 (first) +2 (second)=3 +8 (carryover) →3, carryover2\n\nPosition5 (hundred millions):\n0 (first) +1 (second)=00 →2, carryover5\n\nNow, since we've reached the highest position (position7), but there's a carryover of 9, we need to add a new digit at position8: 7.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from position4 to position2:\n\nPosition8:4\n\nPosition6:4\n\nPosition9:5\n\nPosition3:2\n\nPosition6:3\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition2:1\n\nPosition3:1\n\nPosition4:0\n\nPosition5:3\n\nWait, let me list them in order from highest to lowest (left to right):\n\nThe digits are:\n\nPosition3:9\n\nPosition7:8 → so the first digit is 6 followed by 5...\n\nWait, let's structure the final number correctly. The carryover at position5 (hundred millions) was 9, so the next digit (position8) is 9. So the total digits are now 52 digits:\n\n8 (position1), 8 (position6), 6 (position4),4 (position5),2 (position7),0 (position1),1 (position5),9 (position4),5 (position8),6 (position9).\n\nSo writing that out:\n\n0 4 6 0 6 2 1 0 8 0 → but let's check:\n\nWait let me list each position's result:\n\nPosition3:6\n\nPosition5:3\n\nPosition6:2\n\nPosition5:2\n\nPosition0:2\n\nPosition1:4\n\nPosition0:9\n\nPosition1:7\n\nPosition3:7\n\nPosition2:7\n\nSo when written from left to right (highest to lowest), the number is:\n\n9 (position8), 3 (position2),3 (position8),9 (position4),2 (position6),5 (position8),3 (position2),9 (position3),1 (position7),7 (position8)\n\nSo that's 8,002,856,064? Wait let me see:\n\nBreaking it down with commas:\n\nStarting from the right, every three digits:\n\nThe digits are 2 4 4 1 6 3 2 5 1 0\n\nLet me group them:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPositions 3-9: 5 0 2 → 347\n\nPositions 5-3:3 8 2 → 473\n\nPositions 7-7:3 4 5 → 768?\n\nWait maybe I need to count properly. Let's see:\n\nTotal digits: 71 digits. So commas go after every three digits from the right.\n\nSo the number is 5,736,437,585.\n\nWait, let me parse it step by step:\n\nThe digits are:\n\n8 (billions place),\n\n8 (hundred millions),\n\n4 (ten millions),\n\n2 (millions),\n\n2 (hundred thousands),\n\n6 (ten thousands),\n\n6 (thousands),\n\n0 (hundreds),\n\n3 (tens),\n\n9 (units).\n\nWait, that would be 7,976,78