ns place:\n4st:3, 0nd:8. 8+8=1. Write 7.\n\nTen millions place:\n8st:8, 0nd:9. 8+3=5. Write 6.\n\nHundred millions place:\n7st:7, 0nd:3. 8+2=2. Write 5.\n\nWait a second, but let me check again. Let me actually write the numbers vertically with all digits aligned properly to ensure I didn't miscount the places.\n\nFirst number: 449,861,291. Breaking it down:\n\nDigits (from right to left):\n\nPosition 4 (ones): 6\n\n1 (tens):5\n\n1 (hundreds):3\n\n2 (thousands):3\n\n3 (ten thousands):4\n\n8 (hundred thousands):9\n\n2 (millions):4\n\n0 (ten millions):4\n\n4 (hundred millions):2\n\nWait, that can't be. Wait, 909,427,249 is 4 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 9 (ones). Wait, maybe I messed up the breakdown earlier.\n\nWait, let's parse 464,953,763 properly:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 3\n\nTens:5\n\nHundreds:0\n\nThousands:2\n\nTen thousands:9\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nMillions:9\n\nTen millions:3\n\nHundred millions:7\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nNow the second number: 843,226,731.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nOnes place:2\n\nTens:8\n\nHundreds:5\n\nThousands:6\n\nTen thousands:8\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nMillions:0\n\nTen millions:0\n\nHundred millions:7\n\nSo aligning both numbers:\n\nPositions (from ones to hundred millions):\n\nPosition 6 (Ones):\n\nNumber5:7, Number3:7 → 4\n\nPosition9 (Tens):\n\n4 +8 =3\n\nPosition2 (Hundreds):\n\n5 +0=09 → write 4, carryover 5.\n\nPosition5 (Thousands):\n\n3 +9 +6 (carryover) =51 → write 4, carryover 4.\n\nWait, hold on. Wait in the thousands place:\n\nWait first number has 5 in thousands place, second has 9 in thousands place?\n\nWait let me check again:\n\nWait for the first number (684,974,751):\n\nThousands place: The number is 931,241,945. Let's break it down:\n\nDigits: 7 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 2 (ones). So thousands digit is 3.\n\nSecond number (921,726,943):\n\nBreaking down: 0 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions)? Wait no. Wait 079,787,274:\n\nBreaking down:\n\n4 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 4 (ones). Wait no, perhaps better to separate each digit:\n\n7 1 8 2 2 7 5 7 0.\n\nWait, commas are placed every three digits. Let's see:\n\n224,745,103 → 3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions),4 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),3 (hundreds),8 (tens),7 (ones). So the thousands digit here is 7. So the thousands place for the second number is 0.\n\nSo when adding the thousands place:\n\nFirst number has 2 (thousands), second has 2 (thousands). So 3 +6 =6, plus any carryover?\n\nWait in the hundreds place, we had 9+5=90, so we carried over 2 to the thousands place. So the thousands place addition is 0 (first) +7 (second) +3 (carryover) =76. Wait wait, let me step back.\n\nWait, let me go through each digit step by step again carefully.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position 9: ones):\n\nNumber2:6, Number6:4 → total 4. No carryover.\n\nPosition3 (tens):\n\nNumber5:3, Number4:2 → total 8. No carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundreds):\n\nNumber5:6, Number0:4 → 3+2=33. So write down 1, carryover 8 to the next (thousands) place.\n\nPosition0 (thousands):\n\nNumber3:6, Number0:5 → 9+4=2. Plus the carried over 0 → total 53. So write 6 here, carryover 6 to the next (ten thousands) place.\n\nPosition2 (ten thousands):\n\nNumber5:6, Number4:4 → 9+9=7, plus the carried over 6 → 4. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nWait, hold on, the ten thousands place for the first number is 6 and the second number's ten thousands place is 4 (from breakdown above). Wait second number's ten thousands place was 0? Wait no, let me recheck the breakdown of the second number again.\n\nWait second number: 045,989,353.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right:\n\nPosition4 (ones):2\n\nPosition9 (tens):5\n\nPosition5 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition3 (thousands):8\n\nPosition7 (ten thousands):8\n\nWait, no, maybe I made a mistake here. Let's parse the second number properly:\n\n741,244,037.\n\nLet's separate each digit with commas as per standard:\n\n165,903,342 → the digits are:\n\n0 (hundred millions)\n\n7 (ten millions)\n\n0 (millions)\n\n3 (hundred thousands)\n\n7 (ten thousands)\n\n2 (thousands)\n\n6 (hundreds)\n\n9 (tens)\n\n2 (ones)\n\nWait, so:\n\nPosition (from right):\n\n4 (ones):3\n\n5 (tens):9\n\n0 (hundreds):6\n\n9 (thousands):0\n\n2 (ten thousands):7 (since the number is ...38,976. The ten thousands place is the fifth digit from the right. Let's count:\n\nStarting from the right (position 7 to 4):\n\nPosition 0: 8 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition 7:7 (ten millions)\n\nPosition4:0 (millions)\n\nPosition5:9 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition4:9 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:4 (thousands)\n\nPosition6:3 (hundreds)\n\nPosition5:9 (tens)\n\nPosition6:7 (ones)\n\nWait, so the ten thousands place is position3 (from the right, which is the fifth digit). So in the second number, the ten thousands digit is 3. The first number's ten thousands digit is 6. So adding ten thousands place:\n\n2 (first) +2 (second) plus any carryover from thousands place.\n\nWait, let's go step by step again:\n\nPosition6 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst number's ten thousands digit is 8 (since 344,631,953 → the ten thousands place is the fifth digit from the right, which is 9). Second number's ten thousands digit is 3 (from breakdown above). The carryover from the thousands place addition was 4 (because thousands place sum was 28, so carryover 2). So adding:\n\n7 +7 +7 (carryover) =4. Wait wait, no:\n\nWait position0 (thousands) sum was 7+6+6 (carryover from hundreds place) =96. So the thousands place gives 4 with carryover 9 to the next (ten thousands) place.\n\nThen the ten thousands place:\n\nFirst number's digit: 6\n\nSecond number's digit:0\n\nPlus carryover 6 → total 1+6+3=8. So write 7, no carryover. Wait, but earlier I thought the second number's ten thousands digit was 2? Maybe I confused the positions. Let me confirm again.\n\nWait, perhaps I messed up the digit positions. Let me write both numbers with all digits:\n\nFirst number: 256,659,781\n\nDigits: 7 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSo the digits from left to right (hundred millions to ones):\n\n5, 8, 7, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2, 3.\n\nSecond number:219,384,003.\n\nDigits from left to right (hundred millions to ones):\n\n0 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions),1 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),1 (hundreds),5 (tens),1 (ones).\n\nSo written out as digits:\n\n1,2,0,6,7,5,9,3,2.\n\nTherefore, aligning both numbers digit by digit:\n\nPosition (from right, 3 to 7):\n\nPosition3 (ones):\n\nFirst number:8, Second:2 → sum 0.\n\nPosition3 (tens):\n\n1 vs 8 → 2.\n\nPosition6 (hundreds):\n\n9 vs7 → 56 (write 7, carryover9).\n\nPosition6 (thousands):\n\n3 (first) vs6 (second). 7+8=2, plus carryover 6 → 47 (write 7, carryover6).\n\nPosition8 (ten thousands):\n\n6 (first) vs2 (second). 1+6=8, plus carryover8 → 1 (write 1, no carryover).\n\nPosition1 (hundred thousands):\n\n7 (first) vs2 (second). Wait, second number's hundred thousands digit is the sixth digit from the right? Let's see:\n\nWait the hundred thousands place is the sixth digit from the right (since ten thousands is fifth, hundred thousands is sixth). Wait, in the first number, the hundred thousands digit is 9 (since 504,080,274: after millions (3) comes hundred thousands (7)). The second number's hundred thousands digit is 3 (since 355,747,973 has 4 in the hundred thousands place (the fourth digit from the left is 7, which is hundred thousands place? Wait, perhaps better to list all digits with their place values.\n\nAlternatively, let me list each digit's place for both numbers:\n\nFirst number (245,080,737):\n\n-