messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 229804604 and 897947650?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>Okay, let's see, I need to add 547,198,093 and 768,630,363. Hmm, I should probably start by writing them down one on top of the other to align the digits properly. Let me visualize that.\n\nStarting with the ones place: both numbers have a 9 and a 6. Wait, no, let me check again. The first number is 730,433,131, so the last digit is 1. The second number is 048,029,925, so the last digit is 2. Adding those gives 6 + 7 = 2. So the ones place is 7.\n\nNext, the tens place. The first number has a 9 in the tens place, and the second has a 3. 1 + 9 = 6. So the tens digit is 6.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place. The first number has a 7, and the second has a 1. 3 + 4 = 26. Oh, that's more than 41, so I need to carry over. I'll write down 1 in the hundreds place and carry over 6 to the thousands.\n\nThousands place: The first number has a 2, the second has a 5. Adding those plus the carried 0: 8 + 2 is 36, plus 6 makes 61. Again, carry over 7. So thousands place is 4, carryover 5.\n\nTen-thousands place: First number has 2, second has 7. 2 + 9 = 3, plus the carried 5 makes 7. So that's 3 here, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands place: First number has 3, second has 0. 2 +8 is 67. So write 6, carryover 4.\n\nMillions place: First number has 6, second has 2. 5+4=05, plus the 5 carried over is 38. So write 1 and carryover 2.\n\nTen millions place: First number has 4, second has 1. 9+2=7 plus the 3 is 99. So write 1 and carryover 4.\n\nHundred millions place: First number has 7, second has 4. 6+2=57, plus the carried 0 makes 85. So write 2 and carryover 7 to the next place.\n\nBillions place: The first number has nothing here, so it's 9. The second number has 3 in the next digit? Wait, let me check the original numbers again. Wait, 876,331,098 is 3 digits: 6 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 0 (millions), etc. The second number is 121,200,072 which is also 5 digits: 5 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 2 (millions), etc. Wait, so when adding the hundred millions place, first number is 7 (from 503,356,020: the first digit is 7), and the second is 1 (from 356...). Wait, actually, let me get the exact digits in each place properly.\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas to see:\n\nFirst number: 706,613,397 → breaking down digits:\n\n- Hundred millions: 3 (the first digit)\n- Ten millions: 7\n- Millions: 3\n- Hundred thousands: 9\n- Ten thousands: 4\n- Thousands: 7\n- Hundreds: 7\n- Tens: 5\n- Ones: 7\n\nSecond number: 288,108,981 → digits:\n\n- Hundred millions: 1\n- Ten millions: 0\n- Millions: 2\n- Hundred thousands: 8\n- Ten thousands: 9\n- Thousands: 6\n- Hundreds: 8\n- Tens: 7\n- Ones: 8\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake earlier in the places. Let me confirm each digit's position properly. Let me write them out digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 9 5 7 9 3 0 7 5 2\n\nSecond number:2 6 6 7 3 5 9 4 4\n\nWait, actually, let's line them up properly:\n\nLet me write each digit from right to left (starting from ones):\n\nFirst number: 9 (ones), 5 (tens), 8 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 1 (hundred thousands), 1 (millions), 3 (ten millions), 8 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number: 2 (ones),6 (tens),2 (hundreds),3 (thousands),1 (ten thousands),0 (hundred thousands),2 (millions),5 (ten millions),3 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to write them as:\n\nNumber3: 348,230,999 → digits:\n\nPosition (from right, starting at 2):\n\n8:7 (ones)\n\n5:5 (tens)\n\n2:5 (hundreds)\n\n0:8 (thousands)\n\n7:4 (ten thousands)\n\n9:6 (hundred thousands)\n\n3:3 (millions)\n\n9:9 (ten millions)\n\n4:3 (hundred millions)\n\nNumber8: 482,853,883 → digits:\n\n4:6 (ones)\n\n5:6 (tens)\n\n6:9 (hundreds)\n\n1:8 (thousands)\n\n0:0 (ten thousands)\n\n0:4 (hundred thousands)\n\n9:0 (millions)\n\n0:5 (ten millions)\n\n1:7 (hundred millions)\n\nWait, actually, let's make sure:\n\n201,692,179: Breaking down:\n\nDigits: 9 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),1 (hundreds),3 (tens),4 (ones). So yes, the digits from left to right:\n\n9,3,6,0,7,3,9,2,7.\n\nSo when adding, let's align each digit properly:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\nDigit 2 (Ones):\n\n1 (from first) + 6 (from second) =5. Write down 0.\n\nDigit 7 (Tens):\n\n6 +3=3. Write down 6.\n\nDigit4 (Hundreds):\n\n2+1=54. Write 3, carryover 8.\n\nDigit9 (Thousands):\n\n9 +6 =66, plus carryover 8 makes 90. So write 8, carryover 2.\n\nDigit3 (Ten thousands):\n\n2 +1 =1, plus 7 is 0. Write 3, no carryover.\n\nDigit1 (Hundred thousands):\n\n6 +7 =86. Write 6, carryover 3.\n\nDigit8 (Millions):\n\n8 +3 =31, plus 7 is 83. Write 4, carryover 2.\n\nDigit4 (Ten millions):\n\n0 +3 =74, plus 9 is 45. Write 4, carryover 7.\n\nDigit1 (Hundred millions):\n\n7 +9 =39, plus 2 is 03. So here, we have 29. Wait, so that would be writing 3 and carryover 3 to a new billion place.\n\nAh, right, so the sum will have an extra digit. So the total is 3 (billion place), 7 (hundred millions), then the rest.\n\nPutting all together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigits:\n\n2:6\n\n5:1\n\n0:4 (with a carryover 0)\n\n8:4 (carryover 0)\n\n0:9\n\n2:9 (carryover 4)\n\n2:1 (carryover 6)\n\n3:1 (carryover 4)\n\n7:5 (from 4+1+4 carryover? Wait, let me retrace.\n\nWait, let me go step by step again to avoid confusion.\n\nLet me write the addition vertically:\n\n          6 9 3 1 5 5 9 9 6\n        + 0 7 4 8 2 8 4 3 7\n        --------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\nOnes place: 5 +4 =3. Write 1.\n\nTens place:5 +3=9. Write7.\n\nHundreds place:0 +0=45. Write 7, carryover 0.\n\nThousands place:1 +1=92 +6=58. Write 7, carryover8.\n\nTen thousands place:8 +3=3 +8=9. Write4.\n\nHundred thousands place:6 +6=43. Write6, carryover0.\n\nMillions place:8 +2=97 +9=08. Write0, carryover5.\n\nTen millions place:0 +6=86 +5=55. Write3, carryover2.\n\nHundred millions place:5 +4=24 +6=44. Write9, carryover4.\n\nNow, since there's a carryover of 0 after the hundred millions place, we need to add a new digit to the left (billions place): 9.\n\nSo putting all together, the digits from right to left are:\n\n8,2,4,8,7,5,9,8,9 and the final carryover 4.\n\nWait, but when written in order, the digits would be:\n\nStarting from the left (billions place) to the right:\n\n2 (billion), then the digits from the hundred millions place down:\n\nSo the sum is 3 0 1 1 3 7 6 9 1 1 5? Wait, no. Let me see:\n\nWait, let me structure it properly:\n\nThe result digits (from right to left) after each step:\n\nPosition 5 (Ones):5\n\nPosition4 (Tens):4\n\nPosition9 (Hundreds):3 (with carryover)\n\nPosition2 (Thousands):9 (with carryover)\n\nPosition4 (Ten thousands):6\n\nPosition9 (Hundred thousands):8\n\nPosition8 (Millions):5\n\nPosition6 (Ten millions):3\n\nPosition7 (Hundred millions):9 (from 23, so the digit is 6, and carryover 2 to billions)\n\nPlus the final carryover 4 in the billions place.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (ones to billions) would be:\n\n2 (2), 3 (9), 9 (6), 3 (8),3 (6),6 (3),3 (4),7 (7),7 (4),6 (03).\n\nTherefore, writing the number from left (billions) to right (ones):\n\n3 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands?), wait, let me reconstruct:\n\nWait, let me list each digit position:\n\nBillions place: 7\n\nHundred millions: 7\n\nTen millions:8\n\nMillions:0\n\nHundred thousands:3?\n\nWait, perhaps I need to write it step by step:\n\nThe digits after each step, starting from the first digit (ones):\n\nThe final digits from right to left (positions 9 to 72):\n\nPosition 58 (billions):6\n\nPosition2 (hundred millions):9\n\nPosition6 (ten millions):4\n\nPosition9 (millions):6\n\nPosition9 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition9 (ten thousands):0\n\nPosition9 (thousands):8\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):4\n\nPosition7 (tens):7\n\nPosition4 (ones):9\n\nSo when written from left (billions) to right (ones):\n\n9 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions),1 (millions),9 (hundred thousands),3 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),7 (hundreds),1 (tens),0 (ones).\n\nSo the number is 6,415,905,080.\n\nWait, let me check that again:\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nBillions:2\n\nHundred millions:8 → 0,7\n\nTen millions:6 → 5,71\n\nMillions:4 → 3,650\n\nHundred thousands:3 → 8,234,2\n\nTen thousands:4 → 4,619,83\n\nThousands:1 → 2,952,685\n\nHundreds:7 → 4,771,370,8\n\nTens:1 → 6,454,508,36\n\nOnes:5 → 0,932,575,910.\n\nYes, that seems right. Let me verify with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nAlternatively, 387,568,743 + 012,313,491.\n\nLet me add the millions first:\n\n760,147,265 is 704 million plus 561,973.\n\n289,391,872 is 732 million plus 102,173.\n\nAdding the millions parts: 964 + 540 = 8,957 million.\n\nThen adding the remaining parts: 985,363 + 421,466.\n\n625,218 + 132,736:\n\nAdding those:\n\n266k + 094k = 7,021k\n\n5,710 +82,405 = 99,822\n\nTotal: 0,698,437 +66,099 =8,671,522.\n\nSo total sum is 2,874,354,127 +0,011,636 = 5,399,865,305. Which matches the previous result. So that seems correct.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 4,344,421,683.\n\nWait, but let me check once more the digit-by-digit addition to ensure no step was missed.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number: 788,363,037\n\nSecond number: 254,881,378\n\nAligning:\n\n        3 6 3 0 5 1 0 2 8\n\n      + 3 3 4 8 8 1 9 9 6\n\n      --------------------\n\nStarting from the right (ones):\n\n7+1=7\n\n6+4=0\n\n2+5=88 (write 0, carry 5)\n\n2+1=25 +4=28 (write3, carry7)\n\n1+6=7 +4=2\n\n6+6=49 (write9, carry0)\n\n0+3=71 +8=88 (write4, carry6)\n\n7+0=92 +0=27 (write8,