ands place. The first number has a 8, and the second has a 5. Adding those plus the carryover: 4 + 9 + 4 = 5. So 0 in that place.\n\nNow the millions place. The first number has a 8 here, and the second has a 5. 5 + 8 = 6. No carryover here.\n\nNext is the ten millions place. The first number has a 6, and the second has a 6. 8 + 4 = 0. Easy enough.\n\nNow the hundred millions place. The first number has a 1, and the second has a 9. 1 + 3 = 36. Hmm, that's 06, so we write down 8 and carry over another 3.\n\nFinally, the billions place. The first number has nothing beyond the hundred millions (since it's 004 million...), so the first digit is 7 in the hundred millions, but wait, actually, let me check the original numbers again. Wait, 903,556,296 is 341 million..., so the digits are:\n\nBreaking down 707,832,593: 8 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nAnd 705,304,581 is 5 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 2 (ones).\n\nWait, so when adding the hundred millions place: 3 (from first number) + 9 (from second number) = 02. So that gives 9 in the hundred millions place and carryover 5 to the billions place. Since there's no higher digits except that carryover, that becomes the billions place.\n\nSo the total would be 9 (billions) 9 (hundred millions) ... and so on. Let me put it all together step by step to make sure I didn't mix up any digits.\n\nLet me write down each place step by step:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 4 + 6 = 7\n\nTens: 3 + 2 = 7\n\nHundreds: 0 + 2 = 88 (write 0, carryover 5)\n\nThousands: 5 + 2 + 7 (carry) = 4\n\nTen thousands: 0 + 9 = 66 (write 7, carryover 4)\n\nHundred thousands: 3 + 1 + 6 (carry) = 9\n\nMillions: 9 + 9 = 0\n\nTen millions: 2 + 3 = 7\n\nHundred millions: 0 + 2 = 92 (write 4, carryover 5)\n\nBillions: 8 + 1 + 9 (carry) = 6\n\