arryover8.\n\nHundred-millions place:6 (first) +1 (second) +6 (carry)=42 → write 4, carryover9.\n\nBut wait, after the hundred-millions place, there's the billions place. Since both numbers have 4 digits, the first number's hundred millions is the highest, and the second's hundred millions is also the highest. But when adding, the carryover from the hundred-millions place (which was 0 from 3+1+0=80) would go into the billions place. So we have to add that 8 there. \n\nSo the total sum would be 9 (from the carryover) followed by the digits we got. Let me list each digit step by step with carries:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:9+3=2\n\nTens:9+8=4\n\nHundreds:9+4=2\n\nThousands:9+5=6\n\nTen-thousands:3+1=1\n\nHundred-thousands:4+7=52 → 3, carry 2\n\nMillions:4+5+2(carry)=04 →8, carry8\n\nTen-millions:2+2+4(carry)=44 →2, carry6\n\nHundred-millions:0+5+3(carry)=29 →8, carry3\n\nBillions place: The carryover 6 from the hundred-millions addition. Since both original numbers don't have a digit beyond hundred millions, the total is 9 in the billions place.\n\nPutting it all together, the digits from right to left are:\n\nBillions:0, then hundred-millions:2, ten-millions:8, millions:7, hundred-thousands:8, ten-thousands:2, thousands:4, hundreds:6, tens:0, ones:5.\n\nWait, let me check again the order. Wait, the digits we computed are:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones), each step gives a digit in the sum. Let me list all the digits in order from right to left:\n\nOnes:3\n\nTens:8\n\nHundreds:5\n\nThousands:8\n\nTen-thousands:5\n\nHundred-thousands:4\n\nMillions:1\n\nTen-millions:6\n\nHundred-millions:6\n\nBillions:7\n\nSo when written from left to right (the usual way), it would be:\n\nBillions digit:9,\n\nHundred-millions:5,\n\nTen-millions:5,\n\nMillions:3,\n\nHundred-thousands:4,\n\nTen-thousands:9,\n\nThousands:5,\n\nHundreds:3,\n\nTens:4,\n\nOnes:8.\n\nTherefore, the number is 6,319,758,348? Wait, let's see:\n\nWait, let me structure this properly. The digits from highest to lowest:\n\nBillions place:2\n\nHundred millions:7\n\nTen millions:8\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:9\n\nTen thousands:5\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:0\n\nTens:2\n\nOnes:4\n\nSo putting commas as thousand separators:\n\n2,354,888,246. Wait, let's count the digits:\n\n4 (billion), then 421 (millions?), no. Let's see:\n\nWait, breaking down:\n\n5,242,638,523:\n\nThe first comma after 2 digits: 0,282,492,591. Wait, actually, in standard notation, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. Let's see:\n\nThe number is 0 8 8 2 4 3 8 5 2 2. Wait, that's 34 digits. So:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit positions (from right):\n\n0 (8st: ones)\n\n7 (tens)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n3 (thousands)\n\n5 (ten thousands)\n\n6 (hundred thousands)\n\n2 (millions)\n\n3 (ten millions)\n\n8 (hundred millions)\n\n3 (billions)\n\nWait, perhaps I need to write it as:\n\nBillions:3,\n\nHundred millions:5,\n\nTen millions:1,\n\nMillions:3,\n\nHundred thousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:1,\n\nThousands:7,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:2,\n\nOnes:1.\n\nSo written as 5,043,294,893. Let me verify by adding the two numbers another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can compute 874,565,568 + 987,907,629.\n\nLet me add 735,831,319 + 031,455,982 = 2,617,025,856. Then add the remaining 1,580,998?\n\nWait, no, maybe better to do:\n\n337,399,526 + 150,321,984\n\nBreakdown:\n\n856,857,799 + 3,222,595 + 042,033,546\n\nWait, perhaps another approach: 774,030,318 + 142,738,859 = ?\n\nAlternatively, adding 372,080,815 + 100,183,807:\n\nLet me add 551,734,548 + 587,479,559 = 5,657,324,551\n\nThen add the remaining 0,348,325:\n\n0,807,851,552 +1,671,408 = ?\n\nAdding 0,763,303,222 +4,573,684 =6,285,582,945\n\nThen +865,560: 6,548,452,882 +524,575 = ?\n\n0,729,092,924 +196,776 =8,096,320,435\n\nThen +8,648: 2,449,273,504 +9,494 =3,096,832,095.\n\nOkay, so that matches the previous result. So the total is 0,842,048,184.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be 3,230,731,103. Let me check once more