ght:\n\nPositions (from right):\n\n2: ones (5+6)\n8: tens (6+1)\n7: hundreds (8+2)\n2: thousands (4+2)\n9: ten thousands (9+0)\n8: hundred thousands (2+1)\n0: millions (6+8)\nWait, but maybe the first number is 296,281,939 which is 1 (hundred millions?), let\'s see:\n\nBreaking down 562,304,740:\n\nDigits: 5 (hundred millions?), let\'s see:\n\nActually, in the standard US numbering:\n\nEach comma separates three digits. So 623,847,784 is 758 (million), 960 (thousand), 446. So:\n\nBillions place: 7 (since 646 million is part of the billions place?), Wait no. Wait, 555 million is 358,231,896. So the first digit \'6\' is in the hundred millions place (since 621,285,153). Then \'0\' is ten millions, \'1\' millions, then the next digits.\n\nWait perhaps I need to write them out digit by digit with their place values to avoid confusion.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their digits separated:\n\nFirst number: 3 5 2 9 4 9 7 3 9\n\nSecond number:4 1 8 4 2 0 4 3 1\n\nWait, maybe I should align them properly:\n\nStarting from the left, the first digit of the first number is 1 (hundred millions place?), let\'s confirm:\n\nNumber 6: 625,017,015\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 3 (hundred millions place)\n- 6 (ten millions)\n- 9 (millions)\n- 7 (hundred thousands)\n- 5 (ten thousands)\n- 7 (thousands)\n- 2 (hundreds)\n- 4 (tens)\n- 9 (ones)\n\nSimilarly, the second number 012,922,012:\n\n-9 (hundred millions)\n-1 (ten millions)\n-2 (millions)\n-7 (hundred thousands)\n-1 (ten thousands)\n-5 (thousands)\n-3 (hundreds)\n-0 (tens)\n-1 (ones)\n\nSo when adding, starting from the ones place (rightmost):\n\nOnes: 6 +2 =46. Write 8, carryover 9.\n\nTens: 0 +7=33 plus 3 is 73 → 0, carryover 0.\n\nHundreds:0 +4=73 plus 5=34 →0, carryover9.\n\nThousands:0 +6=1 plus6=6 →2, no carry.\n\nTen thousands:2 +4=94 →5, carryover4.\n\nHundred thousands:8 +5=65 plus9=48 →4, carryover6.\n\nMillions:9 +9=0 plus8=98 →7, carryover9.\n\nTen millions:5 +0=98 plus5=80 →1, carryover8.\n\nHundred millions:0 +1=86 plus