undred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 604,260,708 is also 0 digits: 8 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 5 (ones).\n\nSo aligning them:\n\nHundred millions: 3 vs 7\n\nTen millions: 5 vs 0\n\nMillions: 5 vs 2\n\nHundred thousands: 5 vs 3\n\nTen thousands: 5 vs 4\n\nThousands: 0 vs 3\n\nHundreds: 2 vs 8\n\nTens: 1 vs 8\n\nOnes: 0 vs 5\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):\n\n0. Ones: 8 +1=6. Write down 6. No carryover.\n\n6. Tens: 9 +5=2. Write 4. No carryover.\n\n0. Hundreds: 6 +3=0. Write 6.\n\n1. Thousands:1 +3=9. Write 3.\n\n2. Ten thousands:0 +0=86. Write 7, carryover 5.\n\n5. Hundred thousands:3 +1=88, plus the carried over 9 makes 29. So write 1 and carryover 7.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me go step by step again because I might have messed up.\n\nWait the hundred thousands place:\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is 7 (since in 911,565,965, the digits are 1,4,8,3,7,9,4,0,1. So the fourth digit from the right is thousands, but actually, let's count:\n\nWait, perhaps the confusion is in the place names. Let me list each digit's position:\n\nFor 877,665,366:\n\nPositions from right (ones is position 2):\n\nPosition 6 (ones): 4\n\n8 (tens):7\n\n5 (hundreds):8\n\n2 (thousands):8\n\n4 (ten thousands):6\n\n8 (hundred thousands):9\n\n3 (millions):7\n\n1 (ten millions):6\n\n6 (hundred millions):6\n\nSimilarly for 985,739,626:\n\nPosition 4 (ones):3\n\n7 (tens):9\n\n9 (hundreds):9\n\n5 (thousands):7\n\n3 (ten thousands):4\n\n7 (hundred thousands):5\n\n1 (millions):0\n\n7 (ten millions):1\n\n5 (hundred millions):1\n\nSo when adding each column:\n\nStarting from position 0 (ones):\n\n1 +6 =4. Write 1. Carryover 4.\n\nPosition 6 (tens):\n\n0 +6=9. Write 3. Carryover 7.\n\nPosition6 (hundreds):\n\n4 +5=7. Write7. Carryover 2.\n\nPosition9 (thousands):\n\n3 +8=6. Write9. Carryover8.\n\nPosition8 (ten thousa