g the remaining:\n\n434 + 334 = 899\n\nTotal: 786,556 + 021 = 967,321. That\'s correct.\n\nSo total sum would be 5,839,472,863 + 272,464 = 6,022,445,075.\n\nBut my first method gave me 2,821,390,679. So there\'s a difference of 7,764. Hmm, where did I go wrong?\n\nLooking back at the first method\'s step-by-step:\n\nLet me retrace the digit-by-digit addition carefully.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\n273,411,828\n\n+503,847,982\n\nAligning digits:\n\nLet me write them out with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 261,975,406\n\nBreakdown: 6 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 163,019,108\n\nBreakdown: 7 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nNow, adding digit by digit from right to left:\n\nPosition 8 (ones): 4 +9 =7 ✔️\n\nPosition 5 (tens):9 +4=62 → write 1, carryover 8 ✔️\n\nPosition 7 (hundreds):0 (from first number\'s thousands place? Wait no, wait the hundreds digit in first number is the third from the right. Let me clarify:\n\nWait, let\'s list all digits with their place values:\n\nFirst number: 5 2 1 4 1 8 7 8 1\n\nSecond number:1 4 4 8 2 6 2 2 7\n\nWait, actually, perhaps I messed up the digit positions. Let me write each number as a string of digits:\n\nFirst number: 4 5 0 6 9 2 4 6 8\n\nWait no, let me count properly:\n\nThe number 154,133,990 is:\n\nDigits from left to right:\n\nDigit 2: 3 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit 6: 9 (ten millions)\n\nDigit 4:6 (millions)\n\nDigit7:5 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit8:8 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit4:7 (thousands)\n\nDigit9:7 (hundreds)\n\nDigit3:5 (tens)\n\nDigit7:1 (ones)\n\nSecond number 244,955,956:\n\nDigit4:5 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit6:0 (ten millions)\n\nDigit0:5 (millions)\n\nDigit2:6 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit0:8 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit0:8 (thousands)\n\nDigit6:7 (hundreds)\n\nDigit4:6 (tens