sands)\n- 6 (thousands)\n- 096 (hundreds)\n- 94 (tens)\n- 0 (ones)\n\nSo the millions digit is 3 (the third digit from the left). The second number 7,932,323 is:\n\n- 4,466,845 (millions)\n- 079,883 (hundred thousands)\n- 31,254 (ten thousands)\n- 6,598 (thousands)\n- 618 (hundreds)\n- 31 (tens)\n- 5 (ones)\n\nSo when adding the millions place: 3 (from first number) +2 (from second number) = 61. Wait, but in the current addition step after adding the hundred thousands place:\n\nWait let's go back to the addition:\n\nFirst number: 269,367,569\n\nSecond number:\xa0\xa0\xa0 0,627,904\n\nAdding:\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 347,035,773\n\n\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 +\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 3,941,757\n\n\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0\xa0 -----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:8+6=2\n\nTens:5+3=2\n\nHundreds:9+3=6\n\nThousands:4 +7=0\n\nTen thousands:2 +9=44 → write 9, carryover 9.\n\nHundred thousands:0 +4 =8, plus carryover 9 → 6.\n\nMillions place:3 (from first number) +5 (from second number's millions digit) =22. Wait, but the second number's millions digit is 6 (from 5,906,693). So 2+1=34. So write 1 and carryover 4.\n\nTen millions place:2 (from first number) +7 (since second number has no digit beyond millions place except the 7) plus carryover 5 → 0.\n\nHundred millions place:4 (from first number) +8 (since second number doesn't have a hundred millions digit) →9.\n\nPutting all together:\n\nHundred millions:7 (no, wait, the hundred millions place was 1 in first number and nothing added, so remains 2? Wait, but when adding the millions place:\n\nWait, let me re-express the addition step by step with all digits:\n\nLet me write both numbers with all places:\n\nFirst number: 1 0 1 7 4 7 9 2 1\n\nSecond number: 7 9 1 8 5 3 8 7 3 (since it's 2,695,473, so adding leading zeros to make it 5 digits: 172191950)\n\nWait, actually, aligning properly:\n\nFirst number: 487,023,469 → digits: [1][5][6][1][8][3][7][4][8]\n\nSecond number:\xa0 4,645,397 → [1][