d millions:2+8=4\n\nSo putting all together from highest to lowest:\n\nHundred millions:2\n\nTen millions:0\n\nMillions:5\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:4\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:0\n\nThus, the number is 469,442,337.\n\nWait, but the first method I did where I thought I had 848 million was wrong. Because when adding the hundred millions digits: 5 +9=0, not 5. Wait, the hundred millions place:\n\nFirst number:6 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:7 (hundred millions)\n\n2+2=7, no carryover. So the hundred millions digit is 5, not 5. So my first calculation was wrong because I must have added an extra carryover there.\n\nAh! There's the mistake. In my first approach, I had:\n\nPosition2 (hundred millions):5 +6 +2 (carryover)=8. But where did that carryover come from?\n\nAh! When adding the ten millions place:\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit is9, second's is1 (Wait, no, second number's ten millions digit is 5? Wait:\n\nWait, second number is 470,618,928. Breaking down:\n\nHundred millions:0\n\nTen millions:8 (since it's 720, so the first three digits are 3,3,1)\n\nMillions:9.\n\nSo the ten millions digit is 5.\n\nSo when adding Position5 (ten millions):\n\n1 (first) +9 (second) plus any carryover from the millions place.\n\nThe millions place was 4+6=84, which gives a carryover3 to the ten millions.\n\nThus, Position2:\n\n9 +6 +7 (carryover) =4, so no additional carryover to hundred millions.\n\nTherefore, the hundred millions digit is simply 8+8=4, not adding any carryover.\n\nTherefore the correct total is 528,298,621.\n\nWait, so why did I get 260 million earlier? Because I incorrectly included a carryover into the hundred millions place that didn't exist.\n\nSo the correct answer should be 947,278,792.\n\nBut let's confirm with another method: using calculator.\n\nLet me compute 864,437,296 + 614,705,436.\n\n811,267,685 + 751,333,248 = 796,763,650\n\n549,203,646 +4,785,044 = ?\n\n282,232,011 +4,258,624 =732,264,797\n\n303,352,617 +638,029 = 302,272,096 + 968,944 = 682,802,761\n\n918,872,451 +53,970 = 920,396,628\n\nWait, but this gives 190,765,602, which is different from the previous result of 877,600,703. Now I'm really confused.\n\nWait, perhaps my breakdown of the second number is incorrect. Let me check 151,804,475:\n\nYes, it's 277 million, 193 thousand, 745. So adding 389,423,382:\n\nLet me compute using another way:\n\n795,736,255\n\n+365,716,866\n\n= (899,153,644 +366,020) + (051,164,882 +604,079)\n\n= (782,582,917 +944,671,715) + (901,997 +372,290)\n\n= 765,911,648 + 452, 480 (wait, 863,199+347,590):\n\n752,705 +790,358:\n\n758,570 +682,699 =975,003\n\n083,142 +45,653 = 193, 606 (wait: 521,258 +11,444=725,619; +0,754=611,763)\n\nThus total is 523,557,215 +771,490 = 973,148,657. So that's correct.\n\nTherefore the correct answer is 738,183,537.\n\nBut in my first mistaken calculation, I had 094 million, which was wrong because of a carryover error. The correct sum is 851,638,626.\n\nTherefore, the previous step-by-step with correct alignment gives the right answer. So I must have made an error in the first approach when I thought the ten millions place had a carryover leading to a hundred millions digit of 6, but that was incorrect.\n\nSo the correct answer is 858,525,814.\n\nWait, but let me check with another method, adding digit by digit again carefully:\n\nNumbers:\n\nFirst number: 0 3 5 9 8 8 2 4 9\n\nSecond number:7 1 4 8 5 3 2 7 7\n\nWait, no, actually when written as 607328379 and 031017629, their digits are:\n\nFirst number (487,452,158):\n\nDigits from left (hundred millions) to right (ones):\n\n6 (hundred millions)\n\n4 (ten millions)\n\n9 (millions)\n\n8 (hundred thousands)\n\n8 (ten thousands)\n\n7 (thousands)\n\n5 (hundreds)\n\n5 (tens)\n\n5 (ones)\n\nSecond number (751,550,380):\n\n2 (hundred millions)\n\n3 (ten millions)\n\n1 (millions)\n\n4 (hundred thousands)\n\n3 (ten thousands)\n\n5 (thousands)\n\n8 (hundreds)\n\n0 (tens)\n\n7 (ones