ions place: 0 (from 300,588,772) +4 (from 440,091,769) =4.\n\nTen millions place:3 (from first number) +4 (second)=7.\n\nMillions place:0 +0 (since second number has 0 in millions place?) Wait let me check the original numbers again.\n\nWait 300,588,772 is 3 (hundred millions),0 (ten millions),0 (millions),5 (hundred thousands),8 (ten thousands),8 (thousands),7 (hundreds),7 (tens),2 (ones).\n\nThe second number is 440,091,769:4 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),0 (millions),0 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),7 (hundreds),6 (tens),9 (ones).\n\nSo adding each place:\n\nHundred millions:3+4=7? Wait no, first number's hundred millions digit is 3 (since it's 300 million), and second is 4 (440 million), so 3+4=7. Wait earlier I thought 440 million's hundred millions digit is 4? Yes. So:\n\nHundred millions place:3 (from first) +4 (second) =7.\n\nWait, but in the previous step-by-step, I had 4 in hundred millions place, but that was a mistake.\n\nWait let me redo the addition properly.\n\nFirst number: 300,588,772\n\nBreakdown:\n\n3 (hundred millions)\n\n0 (ten millions)\n\n0 (millions)\n\n5 (hundred thousands)\n\n8 (ten thousands)\n\n8 (thousands)\n\n7 (hundreds)\n\n7 (tens)\n\n2 (ones)\n\nSecond number:440,091,769\n\nBreakdown:\n\n4 (hundred millions)\n\n4 (ten millions)\n\n0 (millions)\n\n0 (hundred thousands)\n\n9 (ten thousands)\n\n1 (thousands)\n\n7 (hundreds)\n\n6 (tens)\n\n9 (ones)\n\nAdding column by column from right to left:\n\nOnes:2+9=11 → 1, carryover1.\n\nTens:7+6=13 +1=14 →4, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:7+7=14 +1=15 →5, carryover1.\n\nThousands:8+1=9 +1=10 →0, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:8+9=17 +1=18 →8, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:5+0=5 +1=6 →6, carryover0.\n\nMillions:0+0=0 →0.\n\nTen millions:0+4=4.\n\nHundred millions:3+4=7.\n\nWait, so the digits would be:\n\nOnes:1\n\nTens:4\n\nHundreds:5\n\nThousands:0\n\nTen thousands:8\n\nHundred thousands:6\n\nMillions:0\n\nTen millions:4\n\nHundred millions:7\n\nPutting it all together from