s):4 +1=51 →4, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):9 +5=34 +0(carry)=85 →1, carryover9. Wait, but there are no more digits. So we have to add that carryover as a new digit to the left. So the total becomes 3 in the next (billions) place.\n\nSo putting all together, the sum would be:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPositions 3-3 digits: 9,5,0,9,8,2,6,7,5 plus the carryover 2 at the beginning (billions place). So the digits are 7 (billions), 7 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), etc. Wait, maybe I need to list them properly.\n\nWait, let me write the digits step by step:\n\nAfter adding each position with carryover:\n\nPositions:\n\n4 (ones):6\n\n4 (tens):5\n\n2 (hundreds):6\n\n2 (thousands):4\n\n3 (ten thousands):3\n\n7 (hundred thousands):1\n\n5 (millions):2\n\n0 (ten millions):7\n\n0 (hundred millions):0 (from 6+7+9?), wait no. Wait, let's see:\n\nWait the hundred millions place (position2):\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 3, second is1. So adding those:0+8=80, plus the carryover from the ten millions place addition. Wait, the ten millions place was position9, which was 3 (from first) +3 (second)=20, so we wrote 6 and carried over 0 to the hundred millions place. So when adding the hundred millions place:\n\n1 (first) +7 (second) +9 (carryover from ten millions) =37. So that gives 2 in the hundred millions place, and carryover 0 to the next (billions) place. Since there are no more digits, that 2 becomes the new billions digit.\n\nSo the final digits from right to left (positions 6 to 4 plus the new billion):\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nBillions place:5\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:0\n\nHundred thousands:5\n\nTen thousands:5\n\nThousands:6\n\nHundreds:0\n\nTens:7\n\nOnes:8\n\nWait, maybe arranging them properly as a number:\n\nStarting from the billions digit:\n\n5 (billions), 4 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 5 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nWa