n\n0 + 1 =9\n\n3 (tens) +1=81 → 6, carryover9\n\n6 (hundreds) +9=60 +7=29 →9, carryover9\n\n2 (thousands) +1=96 +6=21 →6, carryover2\n\n1 (ten thousands) +8=6 +8=1 →2\n\n6 (hundred thousands) +5=65 →0, carryover2\n\n3 (millions) +6=28 +3=78 →3, carryover9\n\n0 (ten millions) +7=7 +3=3 →6\n\n9 (hundred millions) +2=89 →6, carryover7\n\nFinally the carryover0, so total is 4,934,797,883?\n\nWait, but according to the other method it's 3,713,326,057. There's a big difference here. I must have messed up the digit positions in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me check the position breakdown again. Maybe I mixed up the positions in the first method. Let me see:\n\nFirst number: 647,105,289\n\nBreaking down each digit from right (units):\n\nPosition9 (units):6\n\nPosition5 (tens):7\n\nPosition5 (hundreds):6\n\nPosition8 (thousands):1\n\nPosition2 (ten thousands):5\n\nPosition3 (hundred thousands):5\n\nPosition5 (millions):7\n\nPosition6 (ten millions):6\n\nPosition0 (hundred millions):3\n\nSecond number:919,334,059\n\nPosition7:6\n\nPosition7:2\n\nPosition3:0\n\nPosition4:1\n\nPosition5:1\n\nPosition1:3\n\nPosition4:1\n\nPosition9:5\n\nPosition3:0\n\nSo when adding:\n\nPosition0 (hundred millions):7 +7 =45, so that's 3 with carryover6. The carryover becomes the billions digit (position0:9). \n\nSo the result's digits:\n\nPosition0:3 (billions)\n\nPosition5:9 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition1:1 (ten millions) +3 (ten millions) + carryover? Wait, no. Wait, the ten millions place (position9):\n\nFirst number's position9 is6, second's is8. 2+5=1. But in the previous step, when we added the millions place (position3):\n\nFirst number's millions (position9) is0, second's is3. 6+2=44 plus the carryover8 from position1 (which was 3 from the hundred thousands place addition) so 94+7=06. So that gives 3 in millions place and carryover3 to position8 (ten millions). \n\nWait, so the ten millions place (position5):\n\nFirst number's digit is1, second's is1. 3+9=6. Then add the carryover6 