ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 0 (ones). \n\nThe second number is 389,839,838. Let's see: 4 (hundred millions), 7 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 1 (ones). So both are 0 digits. \n\nSo when adding the hundred-millions place: first number has 5, second has 3. 8+6=42. So we write 5 here and carry over 2. But since there are no more digits beyond that, the carried-over 4 becomes the next digit (billions place). \n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right:\n\nOnes: 3\n\nTens:6 (with carryover)\n\nHundreds:2\n\nThousands:5 (carryover 8)\n\nTen-thousands:4\n\nHundred-thousands:2\n\nMillions:3\n\nTen-millions:8 (carryover 9)\n\nHundred-millions:3 (carryover 8)\n\nThen the billions place is the carried-over 3. \n\nSo combining all digits: 8,108,851,753? Wait, let me verify again step by step.\n\nWait, maybe I should do this step by step more carefully.\n\nLet me write down the numbers:\n\n       811,988,076\n     +328,786,167\n     -------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes place: 9 + 7 = 4. Write 7.\n\nTens: 9 + 0 = 24. Write 5, carryover 3.\n\nHundreds: 4 + 8 = 5 + 9 (carryover) = 7. Wait, hold on, the hundreds place in the second number is 7? Let me check again. Wait, the second number is 932,346,070. Breaking down:\n\n946,793,875:\n\nDigits from right:\n\n6 (ones), 3 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 5 (thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 1 (hundred thousands), 4 (millions), 0 (ten millions), 5 (hundred millions). \n\nSo the hundreds place in the second number is 2. The first number's hundreds place is 1. So 6 +1 =8, plus carryover 7 makes 9. So hundreds digit is 7. Wait, I thought earlier I had 0 here. Hmm, so my previous step was wrong here. Wait, I need to correct this.\n\nWait, let's redo the addition step by step carefully.\n\nLet me list each digit position with their place values:\n\nFirst number (106,203,994):\n\n- Billions: 1 (since it's a 4-digit number)\n- Hu