't be. Wait in the digit-by-digit steps:\n\nWait position7 (ten thousands place):\n\nFirst number's ten thousands digit is6 (since 219,769,244 → breakdown: the digits are 9 (hundred millions),7 (ten millions),2 (millions),6 (hundred thousands),2 (ten thousands),1 (thousands), etc. So ten thousands digit is2. The second number's ten thousands digit is3 (from 086,799,482 → the ten thousands place is0). So 2+1=6? But in my previous step, I thought it was 8+7? Wait wait, no! Wait hold on! I think I made a mistake here.\n\nOh no! I see the error now. In the digit-by-digit addition, I messed up the place values for the ten thousands and hundred thousands.\n\nLet me re-express the numbers properly with their place values:\n\nFirst number: 099,332,132\n\nBreaking down each digit from left to right (billions to ones):\n\nDigit 7 (billions place):6\n\nDigit7 (hundred millions):3\n\nDigit6 (ten millions):8\n\nDigit3 (millions):4?\n\nWait no, wait commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. Let's parse correctly:\n\nThe number 802,380,071:\n\n- The first comma separates the millions: 548 (million), then 062 thousand, 210.\n\nSo breaking down each digit:\n\nPosition 7 (billions): 1 (since 635 million is 821,937,735)\n\nPosition0 (hundred millions):4\n\nPosition4 (ten millions):8\n\nPosition0 (millions):1 → Wait, no, that's incorrect. Wait the millions place is the fourth digit from the right? Let me count properly.\n\nWait perhaps the confusion arises from the placement of commas. Let me write the number without commas:\n\n938795740 → split into groups:\n\nFrom the right, every three digits:\n\n888 (ones, tens, hundreds),\n\n727 (thousands, ten thousands, hundred thousands),\n\n6 (millions),\n\n6 (ten millions),\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\nWait no, actually:\n\nWait the number is 442,478,212. Let's parse each digit:\n\nDigits from left to right:\n\n9. 9 (hundred millions place, since 588,656,187)\n8. 4 (ten millions)\n9. 7 (millions)\n7. 2 (hundred thousands)\n